{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting of DeBERTa (train loss 0.054, val loss 0.935, best val loss 0.366 at epoch 4), consistent with the initial plan's root cause analysis. Historical experiments show that combining progressive unfreezing, stochastic depth, and EDA augmentation successfully mitigated overfitting in similar small-dataset transformer fine-tuning scenarios. The ensemble weights heavily favor LR (0.65) over DeBERTa (0.1), indicating DeBERTa's isolated predictions are unreliable, which these changes aim to rectify. Modifications target data_processing_and_feature_engineering for EDA augmentation, model_design for progressive unfreezing and stochastic depth, and training_evaluation to adjust early stopping and enable these features.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add EDA augmentation after splitting but before tokenization: generate one augmented copy per training sample by applying random synonym replacement (replace 10% of words per sentence using WordNet) and random swap (swap two words per 30 words). This doubles the training set to 31,716 samples, diversifying input distribution without changing author style. Implement as a function that takes X_train_texts and returns augmented texts, ensuring original texts are preserved. Augmentation must occur before feature extraction and tokenization to maintain consistency across the pipeline. Preserve existing function signatures and variable names; add new arrays X_train_texts_augmented and y_train_labels_augmented.", "model_design": "Implement progressive unfreezing: freeze the first 20 layers of DeBERTa-v3-large for the first 3 epochs (set requires_grad=False for those layers). After epoch 3, unfreeze all layers. Additionally, apply layer-wise learning rate decay with decay factor 0.95 per layer from top to bottom: the classification head gets full LR (2e-5), encoder layers get LR * (0.95^(num_layers - layer_index)). Integrate this into optimizer parameter groups. Enable stochastic depth with drop_path_rate=0.1 in the model configuration; this randomly drops entire transformer layers during training (probability proportional to depth). Use AutoModelForSequenceClassification with added config parameter drop_path_rate. Ensure the model architecture files support stochastic depth (DeBERTa-v3 supports it via config). These changes directly address overfitting by preserving general features and creating model ensemble effect.", "training_evaluation": "Adjust training loop to incorporate progressive unfreezing: before first epoch, freeze specified layers; after epoch 3, unfreeze all and reinitialize optimizer with new parameter groups. Reduce early stopping patience to 3 (from 5) to prevent unnecessary epochs after overfitting begins, since best val loss occurs early. Implement gradient checkpointing (model.gradient_checkpointing_enable()) to reduce memory usage during longer training. Keep same loss function, optimizer, scheduler, and validation procedure. Modify evaluate_deberta to work with changed model state. Save best model based on val loss as before. These changes ensure the training process optimally exploits the new design choices."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting of DeBERTa (train loss 0.054, val loss 0.935, best val loss 0.366 at epoch 4), consistent with the initial plan's root cause analysis. Historical experiments show that combining progressive unfreezing, stochastic depth, and EDA augmentation successfully mitigated overfitting in similar small-dataset transformer fine-tuning scenarios. The ensemble weights heavily favor LR (0.65) over DeBERTa (0.1), indicating DeBERTa's isolated predictions are unreliable, which these changes aim to rectify. Modifications target data_processing_and_feature_engineering for EDA augmentation, model_design for progressive unfreezing and stochastic depth, and training_evaluation to adjust early stopping and enable these features.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add EDA augmentation after splitting but before tokenization: generate one augmented copy per training sample by applying random synonym replacement (replace 10% of words per sentence using WordNet) and random swap (swap two words per 30 words). This doubles the training set to 31,716 samples, diversifying input distribution without changing author style. Implement as a function that takes X_train_texts and returns augmented texts, ensuring original texts are preserved. Augmentation must occur before feature extraction and tokenization to maintain consistency across the pipeline. Preserve existing function signatures and variable names; add new arrays X_train_texts_augmented and y_train_labels_augmented.",
    "model_design": "Implement progressive unfreezing: freeze the first 20 layers of DeBERTa-v3-large for the first 3 epochs (set requires_grad=False for those layers). After epoch 3, unfreeze all layers. Additionally, apply layer-wise learning rate decay with decay factor 0.95 per layer from top to bottom: the classification head gets full LR (2e-5), encoder layers get LR * (0.95^(num_layers - layer_index)). Integrate this into optimizer parameter groups. Enable stochastic depth with drop_path_rate=0.1 in the model configuration; this randomly drops entire transformer layers during training (probability proportional to depth). Use AutoModelForSequenceClassification with added config parameter drop_path_rate. Ensure the model architecture files support stochastic depth (DeBERTa-v3 supports it via config). These changes directly address overfitting by preserving general features and creating model ensemble effect.",
    "training_evaluation": "Adjust training loop to incorporate progressive unfreezing: before first epoch, freeze specified layers; after epoch 3, unfreeze all and reinitialize optimizer with new parameter groups. Reduce early stopping patience to 3 (from 5) to prevent unnecessary epochs after overfitting begins, since best val loss occurs early. Implement gradient checkpointing (model.gradient_checkpointing_enable()) to reduce memory usage during longer training. Keep same loss function, optimizer, scheduler, and validation procedure. Modify evaluate_deberta to work with changed model state. Save best model based on val loss as before. These changes ensure the training process optimally exploits the new design choices."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan focuses on improving DeBERTa's generalization via K-Fold CV and enriched embeddings for XGBoost. Historical experiments show that merging code into a single script (our current state) and using cosine scheduling with longer warmup are beneficial. However, the current execution reveals severe overfitting (train loss 0.05 vs val loss 0.93) and an ensemble heavily weighted on Logistic Regression (0.65), confirming the need for K-Fold CV. Gradient checkpointing and memory optimizations are essential to fit 5 folds within the 6-hour limit. I select 'training_evaluation' because the core fixes (K-Fold CV, cosine schedule, gradient checkpointing, and embedding extraction) are training/evaluation concerns. 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' remains unchanged as handcrafted features work well. 'model_design' is not selected because the architecture is sound; the issue is training methodology.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Replace the single train/val split with 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa. In each fold, train a separate DeBERTa model on 4/5 of the training data and validate on 1/5. Use cosine learning rate schedule with 20% warmup, increase early stopping patience to 6, enable gradient checkpointing via model.gradient_checkpointing_enable(), and set DataLoader num_workers=4. After training all folds, extract both CLS and mean-pooled embeddings from the last hidden layer, concatenate them (2*1024=2048 dimensions) for each sample, then average across folds to produce robust embeddings for XGBoost. This directly addresses the overfitting by training on full data and reduces prediction variance, while the enriched embeddings improve XGBoost performance. Maintain the existing random seed 42 and the rest of the pipeline unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan focuses on improving DeBERTa's generalization via K-Fold CV and enriched embeddings for XGBoost. Historical experiments show that merging code into a single script (our current state) and using cosine scheduling with longer warmup are beneficial. However, the current execution reveals severe overfitting (train loss 0.05 vs val loss 0.93) and an ensemble heavily weighted on Logistic Regression (0.65), confirming the need for K-Fold CV. Gradient checkpointing and memory optimizations are essential to fit 5 folds within the 6-hour limit. I select 'training_evaluation' because the core fixes (K-Fold CV, cosine schedule, gradient checkpointing, and embedding extraction) are training/evaluation concerns. 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' remains unchanged as handcrafted features work well. 'model_design' is not selected because the architecture is sound; the issue is training methodology.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the single train/val split with 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa. In each fold, train a separate DeBERTa model on 4/5 of the training data and validate on 1/5. Use cosine learning rate schedule with 20% warmup, increase early stopping patience to 6, enable gradient checkpointing via model.gradient_checkpointing_enable(), and set DataLoader num_workers=4. After training all folds, extract both CLS and mean-pooled embeddings from the last hidden layer, concatenate them (2*1024=2048 dimensions) for each sample, then average across folds to produce robust embeddings for XGBoost. This directly addresses the overfitting by training on full data and reduces prediction variance, while the enriched embeddings improve XGBoost performance. Maintain the existing random seed 42 and the rest of the pipeline unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm overfitting: DeBERTa trains to 0.054 loss but best val loss is 0.366 (epoch 4), then degrades. The ensemble weights show DeBERTa (0.10) and XGBoost (0.25) underperform compared to Logistic Regression (0.65), indicating deep learning components are not learning robust stylistic features. The initial plan proposes k-fold cross-validation to address this, which aligns with historical experiments where cross-validation improved generalization. The current training_evaluation module needs restructuring to support k-fold training and adaptive weighting. Additionally, the model_design module should adjust feature extraction and ensemble logic to utilize k-fold embeddings. Data processing remains largely unchanged as features are already well-engineered.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Redesign the model architecture to support multi-fold ensemble: modify DeBERTa to output embeddings (CLS + mean-pooled) for each fold, change XGBoost to train on concatenated fold-averaged embeddings, and implement adaptive per-sample ensemble weighting based on model agreement. Specifically, after k-fold training, collect fold-specific embeddings and probabilities, then average embeddings across folds for robust feature representation. The final ensemble will weight each model's prediction per test sample based on confidence (entropy). This addresses the historical failure of single-split validation and leverages the successful strategy of feature fusion in similar experiments.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the single train/val split training loop with 5-fold stratified cross-validation for DeBERTa and XGBoost. For each fold, freeze backbone for 2 epochs then fine-tune all layers with early stopping (patience=3). Extract embeddings from each fold's best checkpoint and average them across folds. Train XGBoost on these averaged features plus handcrafted features. Optimize ensemble weights via grid search on a held-out validation set (not used in any fold). Finally, generate test predictions by averaging fold probabilities. This directly addresses overfitting and follows the successful historical pattern of cross-validation for small datasets."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm overfitting: DeBERTa trains to 0.054 loss but best val loss is 0.366 (epoch 4), then degrades. The ensemble weights show DeBERTa (0.10) and XGBoost (0.25) underperform compared to Logistic Regression (0.65), indicating deep learning components are not learning robust stylistic features. The initial plan proposes k-fold cross-validation to address this, which aligns with historical experiments where cross-validation improved generalization. The current training_evaluation module needs restructuring to support k-fold training and adaptive weighting. Additionally, the model_design module should adjust feature extraction and ensemble logic to utilize k-fold embeddings. Data processing remains largely unchanged as features are already well-engineered.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Redesign the model architecture to support multi-fold ensemble: modify DeBERTa to output embeddings (CLS + mean-pooled) for each fold, change XGBoost to train on concatenated fold-averaged embeddings, and implement adaptive per-sample ensemble weighting based on model agreement. Specifically, after k-fold training, collect fold-specific embeddings and probabilities, then average embeddings across folds for robust feature representation. The final ensemble will weight each model's prediction per test sample based on confidence (entropy). This addresses the historical failure of single-split validation and leverages the successful strategy of feature fusion in similar experiments.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the single train/val split training loop with 5-fold stratified cross-validation for DeBERTa and XGBoost. For each fold, freeze backbone for 2 epochs then fine-tune all layers with early stopping (patience=3). Extract embeddings from each fold's best checkpoint and average them across folds. Train XGBoost on these averaged features plus handcrafted features. Optimize ensemble weights via grid search on a held-out validation set (not used in any fold). Finally, generate test predictions by averaging fold probabilities. This directly addresses overfitting and follows the successful historical pattern of cross-validation for small datasets."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show that the bi-encoder's validation log loss (0.3642) has significant headroom and its ensemble weight is only 0.1, indicating limited discriminative power. The initial plan's proposed changes (larger projection, gradual unfreezing, Manifold Mixup, Stochastic Depth) aim to increase capacity and reduce overfitting. Historical successful experiments used similar capacity increases (larger projection, gradual unfreezing) to improve bi-encoder performance, while avoiding pitfalls like full unfreezing or excessive dropout. The training curve shows a sharp drop then plateau, suggesting overfitting mitigation via Stochastic Depth and Mixup is appropriate. I select 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' as the modules to modify because the model architecture needs capacity improvement and the training strategy needs better regularization.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Increase the projection MLP from 256-dim to 512-dim with a 2-layer bottleneck (1024->1024->512). Remove aggressive early layer freezing (freezing first 12 layers) and implement a differential learning rate schedule: layers 0-12 get 0.1x base LR, layers 12-18 get 0.5x, layers 18-24 + custom heads get 1.0x. Reduce dropout in StyleMoE from 0.2 to 0.1, and add Stochastic Depth (DropPath) with rate 0.1 to the last 6 transformer layers (18-23) by replacing residual connections with stochastic depth during training. These changes are inspired by successful historical experiments that used larger projection and gradual unfreezing to improve bi-encoder discriminative power, while the regularization adjustments avoid overfitting pitfalls seen in failed experiments with fixed high dropout. Preserve all existing model variable names and forward signatures.", "training_evaluation": "Add Manifold Mixup augmentation applied to token embeddings before StyleAttentionPooling with alpha=0.3, implemented only during training by interpolating input embeddings and labels. Integrate this into the training loop without changing validation/testing paths. Additionally, adjust the learning rate schedule to accommodate the differential rates: keep linear warmup (10%) + linear decay, but ensure the optimizer parameter groups reflect the new layer-wise LR assignment. These modifications directly address the overfitting pattern (train-val gap) observed in the execution results, and align with historical successful experiments that used similar augmentation to improve generalization. Maintain all existing evaluation functions and submission generation logic unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show that the bi-encoder's validation log loss (0.3642) has significant headroom and its ensemble weight is only 0.1, indicating limited discriminative power. The initial plan's proposed changes (larger projection, gradual unfreezing, Manifold Mixup, Stochastic Depth) aim to increase capacity and reduce overfitting. Historical successful experiments used similar capacity increases (larger projection, gradual unfreezing) to improve bi-encoder performance, while avoiding pitfalls like full unfreezing or excessive dropout. The training curve shows a sharp drop then plateau, suggesting overfitting mitigation via Stochastic Depth and Mixup is appropriate. I select 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' as the modules to modify because the model architecture needs capacity improvement and the training strategy needs better regularization.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Increase the projection MLP from 256-dim to 512-dim with a 2-layer bottleneck (1024->1024->512). Remove aggressive early layer freezing (freezing first 12 layers) and implement a differential learning rate schedule: layers 0-12 get 0.1x base LR, layers 12-18 get 0.5x, layers 18-24 + custom heads get 1.0x. Reduce dropout in StyleMoE from 0.2 to 0.1, and add Stochastic Depth (DropPath) with rate 0.1 to the last 6 transformer layers (18-23) by replacing residual connections with stochastic depth during training. These changes are inspired by successful historical experiments that used larger projection and gradual unfreezing to improve bi-encoder discriminative power, while the regularization adjustments avoid overfitting pitfalls seen in failed experiments with fixed high dropout. Preserve all existing model variable names and forward signatures.",
    "training_evaluation": "Add Manifold Mixup augmentation applied to token embeddings before StyleAttentionPooling with alpha=0.3, implemented only during training by interpolating input embeddings and labels. Integrate this into the training loop without changing validation/testing paths. Additionally, adjust the learning rate schedule to accommodate the differential rates: keep linear warmup (10%) + linear decay, but ensure the optimizer parameter groups reflect the new layer-wise LR assignment. These modifications directly address the overfitting pattern (train-val gap) observed in the execution results, and align with historical successful experiments that used similar augmentation to improve generalization. Maintain all existing evaluation functions and submission generation logic unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows a severely underfitting model with val logloss ~1.09 and accuracy ~40%, barely above random. The initial plan correctly identifies the root cause: the custom MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier has critical bugs (untrained proj_to_fusion layers, dimensional mismatches) and the hand-crafted features drown out the transformer signal. Similar successful experiments (e.g., 'I will merge the three code snippets...') show that a standard AutoModelForSequenceClassification achieves much better results (~0.3-0.5 logloss). The failed experiment ('I'll merge the data processing...') tried complex ensembles but still suffered from feature engineering noise. Therefore, the most promising changes are: (1) replace the broken custom model with a standard HuggingFace classifier in model_design, and (2) simplify training by removing differential LRs and adding cosine annealing in training_evaluation. Data processing should be simplified to remove hand-crafted features entirely, as they cause the problem.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted feature engineering functions (extract_syllable_features, extract_punctuation_rhythm, etc.) and the associated scaling/merging code. Only keep the text loading, stratified split, and tokenization steps. The rationale from the initial plan is that the 63-dimensional hand-crafted features are low-quality and numerically dominate the transformer output. Similar historical experiments show top performance from pure transformer fine-tuning without feature engineering. Implementation: delete all feature extraction function definitions and their calls, remove the scaler and feature concatenation, and ensure dataloaders only provide input_ids, attention_mask, and labels.", "model_design": "Replace the entire MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier with AutoModelForSequenceClassification from HuggingFace using microsoft/deberta-v3-large, with a dropout of 0.1. This eliminates all custom fusion layers, encoders, and the buggy proj_to_fusion. The initial plan identifies this as critical—the custom model has untrained projections and dimensional mismatches. Successful similar experiments show standard fine-tuning yields logloss ~0.3-0.5. Implementation: import AutoModelForSequenceClassification, instantiate with num_labels=3, and set hidden_dropout_prob=0.1. Remove the custom class and all references to stylo_dim etc.", "training_evaluation": "Increase epochs to 20, set learning rate to 1e-5 for all parameters (remove differential LRs), remove warmup, add cosine annealing scheduler with T_max=20, and keep dropout at 0.1. Set early stopping patience to 3. The initial plan supports these changes to improve convergence. Similar historical experiments (e.g., fine-tuning transformers) typically use a single low LR with cosine decay. Implementation: modify optimizer to use a single parameter group with lr=1e-5, replace scheduler with CosineAnnealingLR(T_max=20), and update NUM_EPOCHS=20, PATIENCE=3."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows a severely underfitting model with val logloss ~1.09 and accuracy ~40%, barely above random. The initial plan correctly identifies the root cause: the custom MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier has critical bugs (untrained proj_to_fusion layers, dimensional mismatches) and the hand-crafted features drown out the transformer signal. Similar successful experiments (e.g., 'I will merge the three code snippets...') show that a standard AutoModelForSequenceClassification achieves much better results (~0.3-0.5 logloss). The failed experiment ('I'll merge the data processing...') tried complex ensembles but still suffered from feature engineering noise. Therefore, the most promising changes are: (1) replace the broken custom model with a standard HuggingFace classifier in model_design, and (2) simplify training by removing differential LRs and adding cosine annealing in training_evaluation. Data processing should be simplified to remove hand-crafted features entirely, as they cause the problem.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all hand-crafted feature engineering functions (extract_syllable_features, extract_punctuation_rhythm, etc.) and the associated scaling/merging code. Only keep the text loading, stratified split, and tokenization steps. The rationale from the initial plan is that the 63-dimensional hand-crafted features are low-quality and numerically dominate the transformer output. Similar historical experiments show top performance from pure transformer fine-tuning without feature engineering. Implementation: delete all feature extraction function definitions and their calls, remove the scaler and feature concatenation, and ensure dataloaders only provide input_ids, attention_mask, and labels.",
    "model_design": "Replace the entire MultiResolutionAuthorClassifier with AutoModelForSequenceClassification from HuggingFace using microsoft/deberta-v3-large, with a dropout of 0.1. This eliminates all custom fusion layers, encoders, and the buggy proj_to_fusion. The initial plan identifies this as critical—the custom model has untrained projections and dimensional mismatches. Successful similar experiments show standard fine-tuning yields logloss ~0.3-0.5. Implementation: import AutoModelForSequenceClassification, instantiate with num_labels=3, and set hidden_dropout_prob=0.1. Remove the custom class and all references to stylo_dim etc.",
    "training_evaluation": "Increase epochs to 20, set learning rate to 1e-5 for all parameters (remove differential LRs), remove warmup, add cosine annealing scheduler with T_max=20, and keep dropout at 0.1. Set early stopping patience to 3. The initial plan supports these changes to improve convergence. Similar historical experiments (e.g., fine-tuning transformers) typically use a single low LR with cosine decay. Implementation: modify optimizer to use a single parameter group with lr=1e-5, replace scheduler with CosineAnnealingLR(T_max=20), and update NUM_EPOCHS=20, PATIENCE=3."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current execution shows severe overfitting: training loss drops to 0.10 while validation logloss increases after epoch 1, best validation logloss is only 0.3872. The initial plan targets this root cause via multi-sample dropout and a learnable mean-pooling head. Similar successful historical experiments (e.g., 'I will merge the three code snippets...') show that a standard HuggingFace classifier with pure transformer fine-tuning can achieve logloss around 0.3-0.5, but the current implementation still overfits. The failed experiment ('I'll merge the data processing...') tried complex feature engineering and ensembles but suffered from noise. The initial plan's proposed changes—multi-sample dropout as a strong regularizer and mean-pooling with GELU activation for richer representation—are well-supported by historical evidence: standard fine-tuning alone is insufficient for DeBERTa-large on this small dataset, and adding targeted regularization and a richer pooling strategy can improve generalization. I focus on model_design (to implement both changes) and training_evaluation (to integrate multi-sample dropout into the training loop), while data_processing remains unchanged as it already uses pure transformer fine-tuning without hand-crafted features (which was a successful simplification from historical experiments).", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the standard AutoModelForSequenceClassification classifier head with a custom head that uses mean pooling over all token embeddings (instead of [CLS]), followed by a GELU activation, a linear projection to 256 dimensions, dropout (0.1), and finally the classifier layer. This provides a richer aggregated representation and extra capacity (as proposed in the initial plan's Change #2). Implementation: subclass DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification, override forward to retrieve last_hidden_state, compute mean pooling over all tokens (excluding padding), apply the custom head. Keep all other layers identical. The model variable name (model) and tokenizer loading remain unchanged. This change directly addresses the root cause of overfitting by using a more robust, non-linear pooling mechanism that captures sentence-level stylistic features.", "training_evaluation": "Implement multi-sample dropout during training: after obtaining the pooled representation from the model, apply dropout K=4 times with different masks, compute logits for each, average them, then compute loss. This acts as a stochastic regularizer on the final classifier (Change #1). During validation and inference, use a single forward pass without dropout. Implementation: in the training loop, after model forward pass, extract the pooled hidden state (e.g., from the custom head's penultimate layer), repeat it K times, apply dropout with training=True, pass through the final linear layer, average the K sets of logits, then compute loss with criterion. Keep optimizer, scheduler, early stopping, and all hyperparameters unchanged as specified in the initial plan. This regularizer will allow training for more epochs without severe overfitting, targeting a validation logloss improvement from 0.387 to around 0.30-0.35."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current execution shows severe overfitting: training loss drops to 0.10 while validation logloss increases after epoch 1, best validation logloss is only 0.3872. The initial plan targets this root cause via multi-sample dropout and a learnable mean-pooling head. Similar successful historical experiments (e.g., 'I will merge the three code snippets...') show that a standard HuggingFace classifier with pure transformer fine-tuning can achieve logloss around 0.3-0.5, but the current implementation still overfits. The failed experiment ('I'll merge the data processing...') tried complex feature engineering and ensembles but suffered from noise. The initial plan's proposed changes—multi-sample dropout as a strong regularizer and mean-pooling with GELU activation for richer representation—are well-supported by historical evidence: standard fine-tuning alone is insufficient for DeBERTa-large on this small dataset, and adding targeted regularization and a richer pooling strategy can improve generalization. I focus on model_design (to implement both changes) and training_evaluation (to integrate multi-sample dropout into the training loop), while data_processing remains unchanged as it already uses pure transformer fine-tuning without hand-crafted features (which was a successful simplification from historical experiments).",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the standard AutoModelForSequenceClassification classifier head with a custom head that uses mean pooling over all token embeddings (instead of [CLS]), followed by a GELU activation, a linear projection to 256 dimensions, dropout (0.1), and finally the classifier layer. This provides a richer aggregated representation and extra capacity (as proposed in the initial plan's Change #2). Implementation: subclass DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification, override forward to retrieve last_hidden_state, compute mean pooling over all tokens (excluding padding), apply the custom head. Keep all other layers identical. The model variable name (model) and tokenizer loading remain unchanged. This change directly addresses the root cause of overfitting by using a more robust, non-linear pooling mechanism that captures sentence-level stylistic features.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement multi-sample dropout during training: after obtaining the pooled representation from the model, apply dropout K=4 times with different masks, compute logits for each, average them, then compute loss. This acts as a stochastic regularizer on the final classifier (Change #1). During validation and inference, use a single forward pass without dropout. Implementation: in the training loop, after model forward pass, extract the pooled hidden state (e.g., from the custom head's penultimate layer), repeat it K times, apply dropout with training=True, pass through the final linear layer, average the K sets of logits, then compute loss with criterion. Keep optimizer, scheduler, early stopping, and all hyperparameters unchanged as specified in the initial plan. This regularizer will allow training for more epochs without severe overfitting, targeting a validation logloss improvement from 0.387 to around 0.30-0.35."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan targets overfitting and generalization issues in DeBERTa fine-tuning. The execution results confirm severe overfitting (val loss spikes after epoch 4) and low ensemble weight for DeBERTa (0.1). The most impactful changes are in model_design (loss function and training strategy) and training_evaluation (SWA and gradient accumulation). Data processing is unchanged because feature engineering is already robust and not the bottleneck. Historical experiments show that focal loss and two-stage training successfully mitigate overfitting, and SWA reduces validation variance. Therefore, focusing on model_design and training_evaluation addresses the root cause without altering data processing.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Change loss function from CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing to FocalLoss with gamma=1.5 and alpha=0.25, keeping label smoothing at 0.1. This addresses the severe overfitting by down-weighting well-classified examples and focusing on hard cases, as validated in similar experiments. Implement FocalLoss as a custom class inheriting from nn.Module, with forward method computing focal loss with label smoothing: loss = -alpha * (1-pt)^gamma * log(pt) smoothed targets. Preserve model architecture (DeBERTa-v3-large) and optimizer settings.", "training_evaluation": "Implement two-stage training: stage 1 (first 4 epochs) freeze DeBERTa encoder, train only classification head; stage 2 (remaining epochs) unfreeze full model. Add gradient accumulation with 2 steps (effective batch size 32) to stabilize gradients. Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) starting at epoch 3, collecting snapshots every 3 epochs, with SWA learning rate 0.5× original (1e-5). This combination prevents catastrophic forgetting and reduces validation variance. Keep early stopping patience at 5, 40 max epochs, and use same data loaders. The changes require adding a SWA callback and modifying the training loop to freeze/unfreeze layers."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan targets overfitting and generalization issues in DeBERTa fine-tuning. The execution results confirm severe overfitting (val loss spikes after epoch 4) and low ensemble weight for DeBERTa (0.1). The most impactful changes are in model_design (loss function and training strategy) and training_evaluation (SWA and gradient accumulation). Data processing is unchanged because feature engineering is already robust and not the bottleneck. Historical experiments show that focal loss and two-stage training successfully mitigate overfitting, and SWA reduces validation variance. Therefore, focusing on model_design and training_evaluation addresses the root cause without altering data processing.",
  "module": [
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Change loss function from CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing to FocalLoss with gamma=1.5 and alpha=0.25, keeping label smoothing at 0.1. This addresses the severe overfitting by down-weighting well-classified examples and focusing on hard cases, as validated in similar experiments. Implement FocalLoss as a custom class inheriting from nn.Module, with forward method computing focal loss with label smoothing: loss = -alpha * (1-pt)^gamma * log(pt) smoothed targets. Preserve model architecture (DeBERTa-v3-large) and optimizer settings.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement two-stage training: stage 1 (first 4 epochs) freeze DeBERTa encoder, train only classification head; stage 2 (remaining epochs) unfreeze full model. Add gradient accumulation with 2 steps (effective batch size 32) to stabilize gradients. Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) starting at epoch 3, collecting snapshots every 3 epochs, with SWA learning rate 0.5× original (1e-5). This combination prevents catastrophic forgetting and reduces validation variance. Keep early stopping patience at 5, 40 max epochs, and use same data loaders. The changes require adding a SWA callback and modifying the training loop to freeze/unfreeze layers." 
